course: dictionary
lesson: 9764
title: Words that show doubt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you that a claim has not been checked. «Supposedly» is one of them. It shows that people say something is true, or that something is meant to be a certain way, but nobody has proved it. «The house is supposedly haunted.» The word tells you that people say the house has ghosts, but the sentence does not say it is true.
explain: Look at the words around it. In «a supposedly easy test», the test is meant to be easy. The word warns you that it may turn out to be hard. Always ask: is this a fact, or is it what someone says?
explain: The example sentence is «telling someone to "go back" where they supposedly came from is racist bullying and should always be reported». Here «supposedly» shows that the bully's claim about where someone came from is not a fact. The sentence then tells you what the bullying is and that it should be reported.
fact: «Supposedly» is an adverb.
fact: «Supposedly» means according to what people say or believe, though it may not be true.
fact: «Supposedly» can also mean as something is meant or intended to be, as in «a supposedly easy test».
fact: Writers use «supposedly» to show doubt, so the reader knows a claim has not been proved.
fact: Words like «allegedly» and «it is said» do a similar job.
exercise: «The house is supposedly haunted.» What does supposedly mean here? | Empty and quiet at night | Very dark and scary, with ghosts for sure | Certainly true, and proved by a scientist | *According to what people say, though it may not be true
exercise: «a supposedly easy test» What does this phrase tell you? | The test is definitely easy and everyone has checked it | The test is easy because the teacher said so | *The test is meant to be easy, but it may not be | The test is very hard and long
exercise: «The medicine supposedly cures colds.» Find and copy the word that shows the claim has not been proved. | colds | medicine | cures | *supposedly
test: «The castle is supposedly the oldest in the town.» What does supposedly tell the reader? | The town has no old buildings at all | *The claim is said by people but has not been checked | The castle is definitely the oldest in the town | The castle is new and was built last year
test: Which phrase gives the same meaning as supposedly in «a supposedly easy test»? | certainly | proven to be | *meant to be | never
exam: A news report says, «The new drink supposedly helps you sleep.» What should a careful reader do? | Believe it, because it is on the news | *Notice the claim is not proved and check it before believing it | Stop reading the news for good | Buy the drink at once
